#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build and run ent-lang programs.

  ent build program.ent [-o program] [-I dir] [more.c ...]
  ent run   program.ent [-I dir] [more.c ...] [-- arguments]

A program is a .ent file with `main` and the modules it imports. Modules
are found next to the importing file, in the -I directories and in the
devices that come with the compiler.

What a module `m.ent` declares `extern` is implemented next to it, in any
of these, which are built and linked with the program:

  m.c      C, compiled against the program's generated headers:
           ent_extern.h (the extern fns and procs, without the world) and
           ent_world.h (everything)
  m.cpp    C++, the same way (the headers are `extern "C"`)
  m.o m.a  an object or archive built elsewhere, in any language that can
           define C functions; linked as it is
  m.build  one shell command that builds such an object or archive. It
           runs in the module's directory with ENT_OUTPUT (the file to
           write), ENT_INCLUDE (the directory of the generated headers),
           ENT_CC and ENT_CXX (the C and C++ compilers used here) and
           ENT_CPU (the processor flags of ENT_CFLAGS, below) set;
           it may write to ENT_LINK what it needs linked, as m.link
           says it (examples/mojo).
  m.emit   with m.build: one shell command, run as m.build is, that
           writes the module's LLVM IR (text or bitcode) to ENT_OUTPUT
           instead, which the check of its extern fns reads (below), with
           ENT_CLANG set as well (the clang whose LLVM reads it, for a
           language made into C: examples/goose). A crate (m.build next
           to a Cargo.toml) needs none.

The program itself is compiled by the LLVM its IR was made with. The C and
C++ next to modules, and the link, go to the machine's own compiler, which
knows where the machine's libraries are: `cc` and `c++`, or what ENT_CC and
ENT_CXX name; on a Mac, Homebrew's include and lib are added to where
they look (after CPATH and LIBRARY_PATH). ENT_CFLAGS has more flags for
compiling the program itself (-march=native, say); those of them that
name the processor (-march=, -mcpu=, -mtune=) are the modules' too.

Systems that run one after another in a schedule and only work on the
entities they go through are made into one loop over each kind of entity,
on one core as on all: each entity goes through all of them before the
next (fewer passes over the entities, the same result).

The program's own fns, systems and schedules are internal to it (LLVM
inlines them more readily): all but `main` and what the C linked with it
names, which keep their names.

A big program is optimised whole and then made into machine code in pieces,
on a core each: one piece for every two megabytes of its LLVM IR, up to
sixteen. ENT_PIECES says how many instead (1: all in one).

With --parallel the program runs its systems on all cores (OpenMP): a
thread on each CPU it may run on (`taskset` says which; each hardware
thread of a core), bound to it, but for the others of the first one's
core, where one waiting for work would slow the first, which runs what
the program does on one core between its loops (not with --ranks, whose
processes would bind theirs to the same CPUs; on a Mac, where threads
cannot be bound, a thread for each performance core). A thread without work waits five milliseconds for
more and then sleeps, so that it is awake for the next parallel loop
after a short stretch on one core, and what the program does on one core
for longer (drawing, say) has the machine to itself (on a Mac, and in
a program with a window or the http device, it sleeps at once). OMP_NUM_THREADS,
OMP_PLACES, OMP_PROC_BIND or KMP_AFFINITY, and KMP_BLOCKTIME or
OMP_WAIT_POLICY, where the program runs, say otherwise. A loop runs on many threads from a million entities on (fewer
where its body calls out or has a loop of a count known as it runs, or loops
over other entities or over the rows of a buffer, so many times fewer), and the events a reactive one walks from 16384
on; what was sent from loops in its body may be combined on many threads
from 16384 values on, where that has taken less so far than on one (the
program times both ways as it runs; ENT_COMBINE=groups or one, where it
runs, says which instead), the edges connected there added from 1024 on.
ENT_PARALLEL_MIN, where it is built, says from how many instead of a
million and 16384, the others following (for tests: 1, every loop that
can).

With --gpu the `for`s of `gpu system`s run on the machine's NVIDIA GPU
(docs/gpu.md): made into kernels for it (its kind from nvidia-smi, or
ENT_GPU_CHIP, `sm_89` say), which its driver compiles when the program
starts, with a copy of the world on the GPU, the copies between the two
placed by the compiler where a run needs what the other side wrote. It needs
the CUDA driver (libcuda) where the program is built and where it runs,
and cuda.h (CUDA_PATH, else /opt/cuda) where it is built. Without --gpu
those systems run on the CPU, with the same results. An index that only
gpu systems read and whose key only they write is made on the GPU too
(docs/indexes.md); ENT_INDEXES_ON_CPU=1, where it is built, makes every
one on the CPU instead (to compare).

With --ranks N the program is built for many ranks (docs/ranks.md): N
processes started by MPI's mpirun (`run`), each with a world of its own,
which say what one process says; what a device does (an extern proc)
rank 0 does, and every rank is given what it gave back. It needs MPI
(mpicc, mpirun on the PATH; Open MPI or MPICH) where it is built and run.
A program built so is started as `mpirun -n N program`. With
--partitions (as many as --ranks) its distributed entities are placed
when they are spawned, each rank building only its own: the setup too
runs spread over the ranks (ENT_BLOCK, where it runs, says how many
values of what an archetype is distributed by go to a partition).

A program's world lives in memory, as large as its capacities say (rows it
never uses cost address space only). With ENT_WORLD_DIR set where it runs,
it is a file in that directory instead (on a disk), which the system pages:
a world larger than the machine's memory runs, slowly (devices/memory.c).

Files on the command line (.c, .cpp, .o, .a) are taken the same way.

A program can be built for what it does when it runs (profile-guided):
built with --instrument, it counts where it goes each time it runs, into
the folder `program.profile` where it was built from (LLVM_PROFILE_FILE,
where it runs, says another file). Built with --profile program.profile
(that folder, or a file of LLVM's .profraw or .profdata) it is optimised
by those counts: what runs often inlined and laid out to run straight
through. Runs of the instrumented program that do what the program is
for are the ones to count; the C next to modules is not counted.

The machine code says which line of which .ent each instruction was made
from (line tables, which change no instruction): `perf report --sort
srcline` gives the time of each line of the program, and a debugger the
line it stopped at.

Built with --trace, the program times each system, schedule and sort of a
relation, and each parallel loop with each thread's time in it
(devices/trace.c). When it ends it says on stderr where the time went
(each one's own time, and how much of a loop's time the threads worked)
and writes the timeline of every thread to `program.trace.json` where it
runs (ENT_TRACE says where instead; `%p` in it, the process's number),
for ui.perfetto.dev or chrome://tracing to show.

A loop over the entities of a `for` that ran on one thread is timed too,
`(one thread)`; each loop over entities has a site in the trace (its
place, function and archetype). Built with --tune and such a trace, each
of those loops runs on many threads where one call of it took at least
20 microseconds of its threads' time there (TUNE_WORTH_THREADS_US), on
one where less, whatever the rules that guess it say; and one over a
query's slices (a few pieces, at most 1024) deals them out to whichever
thread is free where it ran so, or its threads worked less than 90% of
its time. A grid index made again is put in order anew where more of
its rows than one in K are out of order, K as that took less than each
row moved down to its place in the trace (both are timed where the
traced program runs with ENT_TRACE_EXPLORE=1, one way and the other in
turn; 64 without). What a place sends from a loop is combined by groups of its
targets on many threads from as many values on as that took less for
each value in the trace, one group after another where fewer, without
timing the two ways as it runs. What the program does is the same
either way.

Where a `for` visits every entity to find those whose field has a value
(`where b.team == t`, `where d.stage == Stage.Mild || ..`) and an index
the program does not declare would find them, a remark at the `for`
names the index (`index BugByTeam of Bug by team hash`, a partition of
an enum, a bool or a u8); at a `for ... by` a field that more such
`for`s go by than systems change (`index DotByKey of Dot by key`, a
sorted index that keeps them in order); at a loop over edges `by` a
field of theirs or `where` the other end is an entity (an index of the
relation); at a `for` inside a `for` that
finds the entities within r of a place (`(b.x - a.x) * (b.x - a.x) + ..
<= r * r`) and only counts or gathers whole numbers of them (`index
PNear of P grid (x, y) cell r`, and the `near` it would read); and one
at an index nothing reads, which is not made. With --infer-indexes those indexes are made, as if the
program declared them (docs/indexes.md, step 12); --no-index-remarks
leaves the remarks out. With --tune and a trace, only the `for`s whose
loops took at least 1% of the traced run are named, each with its time.

An extern fn is to give the same result for the same arguments and do
nothing else. The C of the modules (.c, .cpp) is compiled to LLVM IR once
more and read, and so is the Rust of a module whose .build is next to a
Cargo.toml (by cargo, in $ENT_CACHE-rust or ~/.cache/ent-rust), and the
IR a module's .emit writes (Mojo's, say), optimised: a warning
at an extern fn's declaration says where it writes or reads memory other
than its arguments, or calls what cannot be seen into (memory had and
given back, and a way out that does not return, aside), unless the C
marks it ENT_SAME_RESULT, or a static or function ent_same_result_<its
C name> vouches for it (not for --windows).
Built with --check-externs, the program calls each extern fn twice where
it calls it, and compares the results with each other and with what the
same arguments gave before; where they differ it says so and stops. It
also names the extern fns whose C could not be read (an object, a
module's .build).

A module that needs libraries says so in `m.link`: the linker's arguments
for them, as on a command line (`-lraylib`, `-L/opt/x/lib -lx`).

A file a program needs when it runs is declared where it is used (`asset
"name.ttf"`, next to the file that says so). `ent build` copies those
files to where the program is written, where it finds them; with --embed
they are put into the program itself instead, which is then all there is
to take along.

A program that was built before is not built again while nothing it is
made of has changed: its files and its modules', what implements them, the
compiler and the flags. The built programs are kept in $ENT_CACHE, else in
$XDG_CACHE_HOME/ent or ~/.cache/ent, the 64 that were last used. --no-cache
builds anew and keeps nothing.
"""

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))

# What implements a module's extern declarations, next to its .ent file.
IMPLEMENTATIONS = (".c", ".cpp", ".o", ".a", ".build")


def find_tool(name, directories):
    for directory in directories:
        path = os.path.join(directory, name)
        if os.path.isfile(path) and os.access(path, os.X_OK):
            return path
    path = shutil.which(name)
    if path:
        return path
    sys.exit(f"ent: cannot find '{name}' (looked in {', '.join(directories)} "
             "and PATH)")


def llvm_directories():
    prefixes = [os.environ.get("LLVM_PREFIX"),
                os.path.join(ROOT, "build/toolchain/.pixi/envs/default"),
                "/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm"]
    return [os.path.join(prefix, "bin") for prefix in prefixes if prefix]


def homebrew():
    """Where Homebrew is: $HOMEBREW_PREFIX, else where it installs to on
    Apple's chips and on Intel."""
    prefixes = [os.environ.get("HOMEBREW_PREFIX"), "/opt/homebrew",
                "/usr/local"]
    return list(dict.fromkeys(prefix for prefix in prefixes if prefix))


def host_compiler(variable, names, fallback):
    """The machine's own compiler, which knows where its libraries are:
    $ENT_CC / $ENT_CXX, else `cc` / `c++`, else LLVM's."""
    chosen = os.environ.get(variable)
    if chosen:
        return chosen
    for name in names:
        path = shutil.which(name)
        if path:
            return path
    return find_tool(fallback, llvm_directories())


# (The machine a --windows build is for, as clang names it.)
WINDOWS = "x86_64-w64-windows-gnu"
# (And a --web build's: WebAssembly of 32-bit addresses, with Emscripten's
# C library.)
WEB = "wasm32-unknown-emscripten"
# (The most of a web page's 4 GB its world may take: the rest is for the
# program's stack, its texts and what its devices take.)
WEB_WORLD_MOST = 3 << 30


def emscripten(tool):
    """Emscripten's emcc or em++: ENT_CC/ENT_CXX, on the PATH, or in
    build/emsdk (the Emscripten SDK installed there)."""
    found = shutil.which(tool) or os.path.join(
        ROOT, "build", "emsdk", "upstream", "emscripten", tool)
    if not os.path.isfile(found):
        sys.exit(f"ent: --web: no {tool} on the PATH or in build/emsdk "
                 "(the Emscripten SDK) and no ENT_CC")
    return found


def raylib_web():
    """Where raylib built for the web is (its src: the headers and
    libraylib.web.a made with PLATFORM=PLATFORM_WEB): ENT_RAYLIB_WEB, else
    build/raylib/src."""
    return os.environ.get("ENT_RAYLIB_WEB") or os.path.join(
        ROOT, "build", "raylib", "src")


def web_font():
    """The font a window's `label` and `paragraph` draw with on a web page,
    where there are no fonts of the machine's: the one the window would
    find here (ENT_WINDOW_FONT, else the first of those most machines
    have), put in the page as /ent/plain.ttf. None where there is none
    (or ENT_WINDOW_FONT=none): the window library's own letters."""
    asked = os.environ.get("ENT_WINDOW_FONT")
    if asked:
        return asked if asked != "none" and os.path.isfile(asked) else None
    known = ["/usr/share/fonts/liberation/LiberationSans-Regular.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/liberation/LiberationSans-Regular.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/liberation-sans/LiberationSans-Regular.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/TTF/DejaVuSans.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/dejavu-sans-fonts/DejaVuSans.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/noto/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
             "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/noto/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
             "/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Arial.ttf",
             "/Library/Fonts/Arial.ttf"]
    return next((path for path in known if os.path.isfile(path)), None)


def touch_keys(said):
    """The keys of --touch-keys, as the page (devices/web.html) names
    them: `arrows`, `wasd`, Up, Down, Left, Right, Enter, Space, Escape,
    Tab, Shift, Backspace, a letter (upper case) or a digit; each with
    what its button says after a colon, where it says more than the
    key's name (`A:park`; `_` a space)."""
    if not said:
        return []
    named = {"arrows", "wasd", "Up", "Down", "Left", "Right", "Enter",
             "Space", "Escape", "Tab", "Shift", "Backspace"}
    keys = []
    for given in re.split(r"[,\s]+", said.strip()):
        key, colon, label = given.partition(":")
        if len(key) == 1 and key.isascii() and key.isalnum():
            key = key.upper()
        elif key not in named:
            sys.exit(f"ent: --touch-keys: '{key}' is no key a page has "
                     "(arrows, wasd, Up, Down, Left, Right, Enter, Space, "
                     "Escape, Tab, Shift, Backspace, A to Z, 0 to 9)")
        if colon and (key in ("arrows", "wasd") or
                      not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_+./-]{1,12}", label)):
            sys.exit(f"ent: --touch-keys: '{given}': a key's button says "
                     "at most 12 letters, digits or _+./- after the colon "
                     "(and a pad nothing)")
        keys.append(key + (":" + label if colon else ""))
    return keys


def c_compilers(arguments):
    """The C and C++ compilers for the program's own C: the machine's
    (host_compiler), or with --windows MinGW's clang (llvm-mingw's
    x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang, on the PATH), which knows Windows's headers
    and libraries."""
    if arguments.web:
        return (os.environ.get("ENT_CC") or emscripten("emcc"),
                os.environ.get("ENT_CXX") or emscripten("em++"))
    if arguments.windows:
        return (os.environ.get("ENT_CC") or
                shutil.which("x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang") or
                sys.exit("ent: --windows: no x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang on the "
                         "PATH (llvm-mingw) and no ENT_CC"),
                os.environ.get("ENT_CXX") or
                shutil.which("x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang++") or
                "x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang++")
    return (host_compiler("ENT_CC", ["cc"], "clang"),
            host_compiler("ENT_CXX", ["c++"], "clang++"))


def made_of(arguments, known={}):
    """What the program is made of, as the compiler finds it: the files of
    its source and of the modules it imports, the program's first; and the
    files it declares with `asset`, for each name where the file is. (One
    place knows how modules and such files are found: the compiler.)"""
    key = (os.path.abspath(arguments.program), tuple(arguments.I))
    if key not in known:
        own = [os.environ.get("ENT_BIN"), os.path.join(ROOT, "build/bin")]
        translate = find_tool("ent-translate", [path for path in own if path])
        includes = [flag
                    for path in arguments.I + [os.path.join(ROOT, "devices")]
                    for flag in ("-I", path)]
        listed = subprocess.run(
            [translate, "--ent-to-sources", arguments.program] + includes,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
        if listed.returncode:
            sys.exit(listed.returncode)
        sources, assets = [], {}
        for line in listed.stdout.splitlines():
            what, _, rest = line.partition("\t")
            if what == "source":
                sources.append(rest)
            elif what == "asset":
                name, _, where = rest.partition("\t")
                assets[name] = where
        known[key] = (sources, assets)
    return known[key]


def capacities(arguments):
    """What --capacity says, as (name, rows): `Name=N` each."""
    said = []
    for item in arguments.capacity:
        name, _, rows = item.partition("=")
        if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z_]\w*", name) or not rows.isdigit():
            sys.exit(f"ent: --capacity: '{item}' is not Name=N")
        said.append((name, int(rows)))
    return said


def copy_assets(arguments, output):
    """Puts the files the program declares next to the built program
    (unless they are in it: --embed)."""
    if arguments.embed:
        return
    folder = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(output))
    for name, where in made_of(arguments)[1].items():
        to = os.path.join(folder, name)
        if os.path.realpath(to) == os.path.realpath(where):
            continue
        if not os.path.isfile(where):
            sys.exit(f"ent: there is no file '{where}'")
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(to), exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(where, to)


def cache_directory():
    chosen = os.environ.get("ENT_CACHE")
    if chosen:
        return chosen
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_CACHE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cache")
    return os.path.join(base, "ent")


def cache_key(arguments):
    """What says which program a build gives: a digest of everything it is
    made of. None if that cannot be told (a module built by a command of
    its own, which may read anything)."""
    digest = hashlib.sha256()

    def note(*parts):
        for part in parts:
            digest.update(str(part).encode())
            digest.update(b"\0")

    def note_file(path):
        note(os.path.realpath(path))
        with open(path, "rb") as source:
            digest.update(hashlib.sha256(source.read()).digest())

    def note_tool(path):
        # (A compiler is told by where it is and when it was built.)
        status = os.stat(path)
        note(os.path.realpath(path), status.st_size, status.st_mtime_ns)

    own = [os.environ.get("ENT_BIN"), os.path.join(ROOT, "build/bin")]
    own = [path for path in own if path]
    for tool in ("ent-translate", "ent-opt"):
        note_tool(find_tool(tool, own))
    for tool in ("clang",):
        note_tool(find_tool(tool, llvm_directories()))
    for compiler in c_compilers(arguments):
        note_tool(shutil.which(compiler) or compiler)
    note_tool(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    note("embed", arguments.embed)
    note("capacity", capacities(arguments))
    note("windows", arguments.windows)
    note("web", arguments.web)
    note("check-externs", arguments.check_externs)
    note("infer-indexes", arguments.infer_indexes)
    note("trace", arguments.trace)
    if arguments.trace:
        note_file(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "trace.c"))
    note("instrument", arguments.instrument and instrumented(arguments))
    if arguments.tune:
        note_file(arguments.tune)
    if arguments.profile:
        for path in profiles(arguments.profile):
            note_file(path)
    if arguments.gpu:
        note("gpu", gpu_chip(), os.environ.get("ENT_GPU_FEATURES", ""),
             os.environ.get("CUDA_PATH", ""),
             os.environ.get("ENT_INDEXES_ON_CPU", ""))
        note_file(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "gpu.c"))
    if arguments.ranks:
        note("ranks", mpi_flags(),
             arguments.ranks if arguments.partitions else 0)
        note_file(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "ranks.c"))
    note("parallel", arguments.parallel, os.environ.get("ENT_CFLAGS", ""),
         os.environ.get("ENT_PIECES", ""), os.environ.get("ENT_SPLIT", ""),
         os.environ.get("ENT_PARALLEL_MIN", ""), os.cpu_count(),
         os.environ.get("ENT_ATOMIC_SENDS", ""))

    # (The C that keeps what a `for` sends from a loop.)
    note_file(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "sends.c"))
    note_file(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "memory.c"))
    note_file(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "edges.c"))

    include_directories = arguments.I + [os.path.join(ROOT, "devices")]
    files, assets = made_of(arguments)
    directories = []
    for path in files:
        note_file(path)
        stem = os.path.splitext(path)[0]
        if os.path.isfile(stem + ".build"):
            return None
        for extension in IMPLEMENTATIONS + (".link", ".emit"):
            if os.path.isfile(stem + extension):
                note_file(stem + extension)
        if os.path.dirname(path) not in directories:
            directories.append(os.path.dirname(path))
    for path in arguments.sources:
        if os.path.isfile(path):
            note_file(path)
            if os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(path)) not in directories:
                directories.append(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(path)))
    # (The files that are put into the program are of what it is made.)
    for name, where in assets.items():
        note(name, os.path.realpath(where), os.path.isfile(where))
        if arguments.embed and os.path.isfile(where):
            note_file(where)
    # The headers next to them, which the C may include.
    for directory in directories:
        for name in sorted(os.listdir(directory)):
            if name.endswith((".h", ".hpp")):
                note_file(os.path.join(directory, name))
    return digest.hexdigest()[:32]


def cached(key, stem):
    """Where the program with this key is kept, and whether it is there."""
    path = os.path.join(cache_directory(), key, stem)
    return path, os.path.isfile(path) and os.access(path, os.X_OK)


def keep_in_cache(executable, key, stem, limit=64):
    """Put a built program in the cache, and drop the ones that were used
    longest ago beyond `limit`."""
    root = cache_directory()
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(root, key), exist_ok=True)
        target = os.path.join(root, key, stem)
        # (Whole or not at all: another `ent` may be doing the same.)
        partial = f"{target}.{os.getpid()}"
        shutil.copy2(executable, partial)
        os.replace(partial, target)
        entries = [os.path.join(root, name) for name in os.listdir(root)]
        entries = [path for path in entries if os.path.isdir(path)]
        entries.sort(key=os.path.getmtime, reverse=True)
        for path in entries[limit:]:
            shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)
        return target
    except OSError:
        # (A cache that cannot be written is no reason not to run.)
        return executable


def instrumented(arguments):
    """The folder an instrumented program counts into: `program.profile`
    in the folder it is built from."""
    stem = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(arguments.program))[0]
    return os.path.abspath(stem + ".profile")


def profiles(path):
    """The counts --profile names: a file, or those in a folder."""
    if os.path.isdir(path):
        found = sorted(os.path.join(path, name) for name in os.listdir(path)
                       if name.endswith((".profraw", ".profdata")))
        if not found:
            sys.exit(f"ent: there are no counts in '{path}' (.profraw or "
                     ".profdata): run the program built with --instrument")
        return found
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        sys.exit(f"ent: there is no profile '{path}'")
    return [path]


def profile_runtime(clang):
    """LLVM's library that writes an instrumented program's counts (next to
    its builtins, by the name it has on this kind of machine)."""
    builtins = subprocess.run(
        [clang, "--rtlib=compiler-rt", "-print-libgcc-file-name"],
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True).stdout.strip()
    folder, name = os.path.split(builtins)
    if "builtins" in name:
        name = name.replace("builtins", "profile")
    else:
        name = name.replace("libclang_rt.", "libclang_rt.profile_")
    path = os.path.join(folder, name)
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        sys.exit(f"ent: cannot find LLVM's profile library ('{path}')")
    return path


def piece_count(llvm):
    """In how many pieces a program's LLVM IR is compiled: ENT_PIECES, or
    one for every 160 kilobytes of its bitcode (some two megabytes as
    text), on up to sixteen cores."""
    asked = os.environ.get("ENT_PIECES", "")
    if asked.isdigit():
        return int(asked)
    return min(os.path.getsize(llvm) // 160_000, os.cpu_count() or 1, 16)


def mpi_flags():
    """MPI's flags to compile and to link with, as its mpicc says them."""
    mpicc = shutil.which("mpicc")
    if not mpicc:
        sys.exit("ent: --ranks: no MPI (mpicc) on the PATH; install Open MPI "
                 "or MPICH")
    said = [subprocess.run([mpicc, "--showme:" + what], capture_output=True,
                           text=True).stdout.split() for what in
            ("compile", "link")]
    if not said[1]:
        # (MPICH says it otherwise.)
        said = [subprocess.run([mpicc, what], capture_output=True,
                               text=True).stdout.split()[1:]
                for what in ("-compile_info", "-link_info")]
    return said


def gpu_chip():
    """The kind of GPU the kernels are for: ENT_GPU_CHIP, else what
    nvidia-smi says of the machine's first."""
    chosen = os.environ.get("ENT_GPU_CHIP")
    if chosen:
        return chosen
    smi = shutil.which("nvidia-smi")
    if smi:
        listed = subprocess.run([smi, "--query-gpu=compute_cap",
                                 "--format=csv,noheader"],
                                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
        first = listed.stdout.split()
        if listed.returncode == 0 and first:
            return "sm_" + first[0].replace(".", "")
    sys.exit("ent: --gpu: cannot tell the machine's GPU (no nvidia-smi); "
             "say which with ENT_GPU_CHIP (sm_89, say)")


def gpu_passes(chip):
    """From the lowered program (its GPU loops mapped) to kernels: those
    passes that come before the CPU's loops are made OpenMP's, and those
    after, which make the kernels into PTX, embedded in the program."""
    # (PTX 8.0: what the RTX 40 series needs at the least, and every
    # driver of CUDA 12 reads.)
    features = os.environ.get("ENT_GPU_FEATURES", "+ptx80")
    before = ["--convert-parallel-loops-to-gpu", "--gpu-kernel-outlining",
              "--ent-gpu-kernel-names"]
    after = ["--lower-affine", "--convert-scf-to-cf",
             f"--nvvm-attach-target=chip={chip} features={features}",
             "--convert-gpu-to-nvvm", "--reconcile-unrealized-casts"]
    finish = ["--gpu-to-llvm", "--reconcile-unrealized-casts",
              "--gpu-module-to-binary=format=isa"]
    return before, after, finish


def machine_features(clang, cflags):
    """The features clang makes code for with these flags, as LLVM's
    target-features list, on x86 (else None): what a function that is to
    prefer no gathers has with it, since a function's own features take
    the place of the machine's (ent-emit-llvm)."""
    said = run([clang, "-###", *cflags, "-x", "c", "-c", os.devnull,
                "-o", os.devnull], stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True).stderr
    command = next((line for line in said.splitlines()
                    if '"-target-feature"' in line), None)
    if not command:
        return None
    words = shlex.split(command)
    triple = words[words.index("-triple") + 1] if "-triple" in words else ""
    if not triple.startswith(("x86_64", "i386", "i686")):
        return None
    return ",".join(words[index + 1] for index, word in enumerate(words)
                    if word == "-target-feature")


# Headers the devices' C uses, kept in the repository (StringZilla, for
# devices/text.c: see devices/stringzilla/README.md).
VENDORED = ["-I", os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "stringzilla", "include")]


def cpu_flags():
    """The flags of ENT_CFLAGS that say what processor the program is made
    for (-march=native, say): its modules' C and C++ are made for the same
    one, and a module's .build is told them (ENT_CPU). Without them all is
    made for any machine of its kind, as a program to give away must be."""
    return [flag for flag in shlex.split(os.environ.get("ENT_CFLAGS", ""))
            if re.match(r"-m(arch|cpu|tune)=", flag)]


def run(command, **options):
    result = subprocess.run(command, **options)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        sys.exit(result.returncode)
    return result


# A loop over entities is worth running on many threads where one call of
# it takes at least this long on one (its threads' time together, where
# it ran on many): a fork and a meeting at its end cost some microseconds.
TUNE_WORTH_THREADS_US = 20.0
# Where its threads worked less of its time than this, its pieces are
# dealt out to whichever thread is free.
TUNE_BUSY_ENOUGH = 0.9


def groups_from(combined):
    """From how many values on what a place sends is combined by groups
    (on many threads): of `combined`, (values, by groups, time) of each
    combining in a trace, by as many values as a power of two (the way
    that took less for each value, or the only one it went; where neither,
    as the next more), the fewest from which every more goes by groups."""
    classes = {}
    for count, grouped, took in combined:
        if count <= 0:
            continue
        each = classes.setdefault(count.bit_length() - 1,
                                  {True: [0.0, 0], False: [0.0, 0]})
        each[grouped][0] += took / count
        each[grouped][1] += 1
    never = 1 << 62
    start = never
    for size in sorted(classes, reverse=True):
        ways = classes[size]
        cost = {way: total / calls for way, (total, calls) in ways.items()
                if calls}
        grouped = (cost[True] < cost[False] if len(cost) == 2
                   else True in cost)
        if not grouped:
            break
        start = 1 << size
    return start


def tangled_from(remade):
    """Of a grid index, from how out of order its rows are on it is made
    anew (more than one in what this gives below the one before, its
    Name_tangled), of `remade`, (rows below the one before, rows, made
    anew, time) of each time it was made again in a trace: by how out of
    order, as a power of two (one in 2, 4, ..), the way that took less, or
    None where no such has both. Made anew from the most out of order on,
    up to the first where moving each down took less; where none did, from
    as far as the trace has it, but no less than one in 64 (as without a
    trace)."""
    classes = {}
    for below, rows, anew, took in remade:
        if below <= 0 or rows <= 0:
            continue
        each = classes.setdefault((rows // below).bit_length() - 1,
                                  {True: [0.0, 0], False: [0.0, 0]})
        each[anew][0] += took
        each[anew][1] += 1
    measured = {size: {way: total / calls
                       for way, (total, calls) in ways.items() if calls}
                for size, ways in classes.items()}
    if not any(len(cost) == 2 for cost in measured.values()):
        return None
    for size in sorted(measured):
        cost = measured[size]
        if len(cost) == 2 and cost[False] <= cost[True]:
            return 1 << size
    return max(64, 1 << (max(measured) + 1))


def loop_times(path, output):
    """How long the loops over entities of each place of the program took
    in the trace at `path`, all their calls' time on their threads
    together, and how long the traced run was (microseconds), written to
    `output` for --ent-infer-indexes ({"run": .., "places": {"f.ent:l:c":
    ..}})."""
    try:
        with open(path) as source:
            events = json.load(source)["traceEvents"]
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError) as error:
        sys.exit(f"ent: cannot read the trace '{path}': {error}")
    places = {}
    first, last = None, None
    for event in events:
        if "ts" in event:
            end = event["ts"] + event.get("dur", 0)
            first = event["ts"] if first is None else min(first, event["ts"])
            last = end if last is None else max(last, end)
        key = event.get("args", {}).get("site")
        if not key:
            continue
        site, how = key.split("|", 1)
        if how in ("count", "tangle") or site.startswith("send "):
            continue
        # (Its threads' time where it ran on many, its own on one.)
        if event["cat"] != "work" and how != "serial":
            continue
        place = site.split(" ", 1)[0]
        places[place] = places.get(place, 0.0) + event["dur"]
    with open(output, "w") as out:
        json.dump({"run": (last - first) if first is not None else 0,
                   "places": places}, out)


def tuned_loops(path):
    """What `--tune` chooses of each loop over entities from the trace at
    `path`: the loops by their sites (ent-lower-to-loops), each whether it
    runs on many threads and how its pieces are shared out
    ({"loops": {site: {"parallel": .., "schedule": ..}}})."""
    try:
        with open(path) as source:
            events = json.load(source)["traceEvents"]
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError) as error:
        sys.exit(f"ent: cannot read the trace '{path}': {error}")
    seen = {}
    # (A grid index made again: how out of order, which way, and the time
    # of the system that made it, which the event is in, on its thread.)
    remade = {}
    spans = {}
    for event in events:
        if event.get("cat") == "span":
            spans.setdefault(event["tid"], []).append(event)
    for event in events:
        if event.get("cat") != "tangle":
            continue
        args = event["args"]
        around = [span for span in spans.get(event["tid"], [])
                  if span["ts"] <= event["ts"] <= span["ts"] + span["dur"]
                  and span["name"].startswith("system ")]
        if not around:
            continue
        took = min(around, key=lambda span: span["dur"])["dur"]
        index = args["site"].split("|", 1)[0]
        remade.setdefault(index, []).append(
            (args["below"], args["rows"], bool(args["anew"]), took))
    # (What a place sent, before each combining of it on the same thread:
    # how many values, and then which way it went and how long it took.)
    sent = {}
    waiting = {}
    for event in sorted(events, key=lambda event: event.get("ts", 0)):
        key = event.get("args", {}).get("site")
        if not key or event["cat"] == "work":
            continue
        site, how = key.split("|", 1)
        if how == "count":
            waiting[(site, event["tid"])] = event["args"]["count"]
        elif (site, event["tid"]) in waiting:
            count = waiting.pop((site, event["tid"]))
            sent.setdefault(site, []).append(
                (count, how != "serial", event["dur"]))
    for event in events:
        key = event.get("args", {}).get("site")
        if not key:
            continue
        site, how = key.split("|", 1)
        if how in ("count", "tangle"):
            continue
        each = seen.setdefault(site, {"calls": 0, "wall": 0.0, "work": 0.0,
                                      "threads": set(), "ran": 0,
                                      "dynamic": False, "serial": 0,
                                      "alone": 0.0})
        if event["cat"] == "work":
            each["work"] += event["dur"]
            each["threads"].add(event["tid"])
        elif how == "serial":
            each["serial"] += 1
            each["alone"] += event["dur"]
        else:
            each["ran"] += 1
            each["wall"] += event["dur"]
            each["dynamic"] = how.endswith("dynamic")
    loops = {}
    for site, each in seen.items():
        calls = each["ran"] + each["serial"]
        if not calls:
            continue
        # What a place sends, combined by groups on many threads or one
        # group after another: by groups from the fewest values on from
        # which that took less for each value (as many as a power of two,
        # each way's time for a value), else one after another.
        if site.startswith("send "):
            loops[site] = {"combine_from": groups_from(sent.get(site, []))}
            continue
        # (What a call takes on one thread: its threads' time together.)
        cost = (each["work"] + each["alone"]) / calls
        choice = {"parallel": cost >= TUNE_WORTH_THREADS_US}
        if choice["parallel"] and each["ran"] and each["wall"] > 0:
            busy = each["work"] / (each["wall"] * max(1, len(each["threads"])))
            choice["schedule"] = ("dynamic" if each["dynamic"] or
                                  busy < TUNE_BUSY_ENOUGH else "static")
        loops[site] = choice
    for index, made in remade.items():
        many = tangled_from(made)
        if many is not None:
            loops[index] = {"tangled": many}
    return {"loops": loops}


def host_includes(compiler, language, known={}):
    """Where the machine's compiler looks for headers (raylib.h in
    /usr/include, say), which the LLVM that checks extern fns is given
    after its own."""
    if (compiler, language) not in known:
        found = []
        try:
            said = subprocess.run([compiler, "-E", "-v", "-x", language,
                                   os.devnull], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                  stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True).stderr
            listing = False
            for line in said.splitlines():
                if line.startswith("#include <...> search starts here"):
                    listing = True
                elif line.startswith("End of search list"):
                    listing = False
                elif listing and line.startswith(" "):
                    path = line.split(" (")[0].strip()
                    # (Not a compiler's own headers, stdatomic.h say: the
                    # LLVM has its own.)
                    if not re.search(r"/lib(64)?/(gcc|clang)/", path):
                        found += ["-idirafter", path]
        except OSError:
            pass
        known[(compiler, language)] = found
    return known[(compiler, language)]


def memory_of(attributes, places=("argmem", "inaccessiblemem", "other",
                                   "target_mem0", "target_mem1")):
    """What a function's LLVM attributes say it does with memory: "pure"
    (nothing, or reads its arguments; errno aside), "writes", "reads" or
    "hidden" (memory no IR names: malloc's, a library's state); "unknown"
    where they say nothing (it calls what LLVM cannot see into). Only the
    kinds of memory in `places` are looked at."""
    found = re.search(r"\bmemory\(([^)]*)\)", attributes or "")
    if not found:
        return "unknown"
    default, kinds = "none", {}
    for part in found.group(1).split(","):
        part = part.strip()
        if ":" in part:
            place, kind = (word.strip() for word in part.split(":"))
            kinds[place] = kind
        else:
            default = part
    worst = "pure"
    for place in places:
        kind = kinds.get(place, default)
        if kind == "none" or (place == "argmem" and kind == "read"):
            continue
        if "write" in kind:
            return "writes"
        if place == "inaccessiblemem":
            worst = "hidden"
        elif worst != "hidden":
            worst = "reads"
    return worst


def ir_functions(ir):
    """The functions of LLVM IR (text): name -> (defined, attributes, body
    lines); the globals it has (name -> constant or not); and the names of
    those marked ENT_SAME_RESULT."""
    groups = dict(re.findall(r"^attributes #(\d+) = \{ (.*) \}$", ir, re.M))
    functions, globals_, strings, body = {}, {}, {}, None
    for line in ir.splitlines():
        if body is not None:
            if line == "}":
                body = None
            else:
                body.append(line)
            continue
        found = re.match(r'(define|declare) .*?@("[^"]+"|[\w.$]+)\((.*)', line)
        if found:
            group = re.findall(r"#(\d+)", found.group(3))
            attributes = groups.get(group[-1], "") if group else ""
            defined = found.group(1) == "define"
            lines = [] if defined else None
            functions[found.group(2).strip('"')] = (defined, attributes,
                                                    lines)
            if defined:
                body = lines
            continue
        found = re.match(r'@("[^"]+"|[\w.$]+) = (.*)', line)
        if found:
            name, rest = found.group(1).strip('"'), found.group(2)
            globals_[name] = " constant " in " " + rest
            text = re.search(r'c"([^"]*)"', rest)
            if text:
                strings[name] = text.group(1)
    vouched = set()
    for line in ir.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("@llvm.global.annotations"):
            for function, text in re.findall(
                    r"ptr @([\w.$]+), ptr @([\w.$]+)", line):
                if strings.get(text, "").startswith("ent.same_result"):
                    vouched.add(function)
    return functions, globals_, vouched


# (Parts of the names of what languages' runtimes call that is harmless
# as harmless_call says, though LLVM does not see it so. Rust's: a
# placeholder of the allocator, and what a HashMap calls where it cannot
# have more memory, which panics or gives an error back. Mojo's: the
# stack trace an error is given as it is raised, which says where. ent's:
# the block of a text a fn gives back, memory it has and the program
# gives back, devices/text.c.)
RUNTIME_HARMLESS = ("ent_text_new", "__rust_no_alloc_shim_is_unstable",
                    "Fallibility17capacity_overflow", "Fallibility9alloc_err",
                    "KGEN_CompilerRT_GetStackTrace")


def harmless_call(callee, attributes, after):
    """Whether a call changes nothing a fn gives back though LLVM cannot
    see into it: memory had and given back (malloc and free, Rust's
    allocator), or a call that never returns (abort, a panic: the program
    ends there); `after` is the line after the call."""
    return ("allockind(" in attributes or
            re.search(r"\bnoreturn\b", attributes) is not None or
            after.strip().startswith("unreachable") or
            any(part in callee for part in RUNTIME_HARMLESS))


# (An IR value's name, and a global's: %5, %"a b", @g, @"std::x".)
IR_VALUE = r'%"[^"]+"|%[\w.$-]+'
IR_GLOBAL = r'@"[^"]+"|@[\w.$]+'


def ir_uses(name, line):
    """Whether an IR line uses the value `name` (as a whole word)."""
    return re.search(r'(?<![\w.$"-])' + re.escape(name) + r'(?![\w.$"-])',
                     line) is not None


def own_stack(lines):
    """The values of a function's IR that point into its own stack: its
    allocas, and what `getelementptr`, casts, and a `phi` or `select` of
    nothing else make of them."""
    local, changed = set(), True
    while changed:
        changed = False
        for line in lines:
            found = re.match(r"\s*(" + IR_VALUE + r") = (.*)", line)
            if not found or found.group(1) in local:
                continue
            value, rest = found.group(1), found.group(2).split(";")[0]
            if re.match(r"alloca\b", rest):
                words = [None]
            elif re.match(r"(getelementptr|bitcast|addrspacecast|freeze)\b",
                          rest):
                base = re.search(r"\bptr\s+(" + IR_GLOBAL + "|" + IR_VALUE +
                                 ")", rest)
                words = [base.group(1)] if base else []
            elif re.match(r"phi ptr\b", rest):
                words = re.findall(r"\[\s*(" + IR_GLOBAL + "|" + IR_VALUE +
                                   r"|\S+)\s*,", rest)
            elif re.match(r"select\b", rest):
                words = re.findall(r"\bptr\s+(" + IR_GLOBAL + "|" + IR_VALUE +
                                   r"|\S+?),?(?:\s|$)", rest)
            else:
                continue
            if words and all(word is None or word in local for word in words):
                local.add(value)
                changed = True
    return local


def writes_only_own(lines):
    """Whether all a function writes is its own stack: each store and
    atomic change to an address own_stack finds, and no call but LLVM's
    intrinsics, a memset's or memcpy's to such an address. (LLVM's
    attributes may say `memory(write)` of one that only sorts an array of
    its own, where it stores through a `phi` of its allocas.)"""
    local = own_stack(lines)
    for line in lines:
        line = line.split(";")[0]
        if re.match(r"\s*store ", line):
            address = line.rsplit(", ptr ", 1)[-1].split()[0].rstrip(",") \
                if ", ptr " in line else None
            if address not in local:
                return False
        elif re.match(r"\s*(?:" + IR_VALUE + r" = )?(atomicrmw|cmpxchg) ",
                      line):
            return False
        called = re.search(r'\b(?:call|invoke) [^@%]*(@"[^"]+"|@[\w.$]+|'
                           r'%[\w.]+)\(', line)
        if called:
            callee = called.group(1).lstrip("@").strip('"')
            if not callee.startswith("llvm."):
                return False
            if re.match(r"llvm\.mem(set|cpy|move)", callee):
                first = re.search(r"\bptr\s+(?:\w+\s+)*(" + IR_VALUE + ")",
                                  line[called.end():])
                if not first or first.group(1) not in local:
                    return False
    return True


def global_pointers(lines, globals_):
    """The values of a function's IR that point into a global's memory:
    value -> (the global, 0 where it is the global's own, 1 where it was
    reached through a pointer read out of it, as a data stack is through
    the global that holds it). Through `getelementptr`, `phi`, `select`
    and casts, and where the global is thread-local, through
    `llvm.threadlocal.address`."""
    def mutable(word):
        word = word[1:].strip('"')
        return word if word in globals_ and not globals_[word] else None
    derived, changed = {}, True
    while changed:
        changed = False
        for line in lines:
            found = re.match(r"\s*(" + IR_VALUE + r") = (.*)", line)
            if not found:
                continue
            value, rest = found.group(1), found.group(2).split(";")[0]
            point = None
            if re.match(r"(?:tail |musttail |notail )?call ", rest) and \
                    "@llvm.threadlocal.address" in rest:
                named = [mutable(word) for word in
                         re.findall(IR_GLOBAL, rest.split("(", 1)[1])]
                point = next(((word, 0) for word in named if word), None)
            elif re.match(r"load (?:atomic )?(?:volatile )?ptr,", rest):
                address = re.search(r",\s*ptr\s+(" + IR_GLOBAL + "|" +
                                    IR_VALUE + ")", rest)
                if address:
                    word = address.group(1)
                    if word.startswith("@") and mutable(word):
                        point = (mutable(word), 1)
                    elif word in derived:
                        point = (derived[word][0], 1)
            elif re.match(r"(getelementptr|phi|select|bitcast|addrspacecast"
                          r"|freeze)\b", rest):
                for word in re.findall(IR_GLOBAL + "|" + IR_VALUE, rest):
                    if word.startswith("@") and mutable(word):
                        candidate = (mutable(word), 0)
                    elif word in derived:
                        candidate = derived[word]
                    else:
                        continue
                    if point is None or candidate[1] > point[1]:
                        point = candidate
            if point and derived.get(value) != point:
                derived[value] = point
                changed = True
    return derived


def deciding_loads(lines, leaving_labels):
    """The lines of a function's IR that load a value used for nothing
    but to decide whether to leave by a way out (a branch to a block that
    ends in `unreachable`, as a check that has the program started, or an
    assert's, does): reading it is no reason a fn is none."""
    def only_decides(value, depth=0):
        for line in lines:
            if line.lstrip().startswith(value + " =") or \
                    not ir_uses(value, line):
                continue
            branch = re.match(r"\s*br i1 (\S+), label (%\S+), label (%\S+)",
                              line)
            if branch and branch.group(1) == value:
                if {branch.group(2).rstrip(",")[1:].strip('"'),
                        branch.group(3)[1:].strip('"')} & leaving_labels:
                    continue
                return False
            step = re.match(r"\s*(" + IR_VALUE + r") = (icmp|trunc|zext|"
                            r"sext|xor|and|or|freeze)\b", line)
            if step and depth < 8 and only_decides(step.group(1), depth + 1):
                continue
            return False
        return True
    deciding = set()
    for at, line in enumerate(lines):
        found = re.match(r"\s*(" + IR_VALUE + r") = load ", line)
        if found and only_decides(found.group(1)):
            deciding.add(at)
    return deciding


def why_not_pure(name, functions, globals_, seen=None, harmless=None):
    """What a function does that makes it no fn, as LLVM IR shows it:
    which globals it writes and reads, and what it calls that cannot be
    seen into (or that does those). The calls that change nothing it
    gives back (harmless_call) are not among them, but are counted in
    `harmless`."""
    seen = set() if seen is None else seen
    harmless = [] if harmless is None else harmless
    seen.add(name)
    writes, reads, calls = [], [], []
    lines = functions[name][2] or []
    # (A block that ends in `unreachable` is the way out: what it does
    # before, saying why, say, changes nothing given back.)
    leaving, block, label, leaving_labels = set(), [], None, set()
    for at, line in enumerate(lines):
        found = re.match(r'("[^"]+"|[\w.$-]+):', line)
        if found:
            block, label = [], found.group(1).strip('"')
        block.append(at)
        if line.strip().startswith("unreachable"):
            leaving.update(block)
            if label is not None:
                leaving_labels.add(label)
    # (Where the memory a value points to is a global's, or reached through
    # one, as Goose's data stacks are; and the reads that only decide
    # whether to leave, as a check that the program has started does.)
    derived = global_pointers(lines, globals_)
    deciding = deciding_loads(lines, leaving_labels)

    def memory(word):
        """The global memory an address names: g, *g (through g), or
        None."""
        word = word.rstrip(",")
        if word.startswith("@") and word[1:].strip('"') in globals_ and \
                not globals_[word[1:].strip('"')]:
            return word[1:].strip('"')
        if word in derived:
            return ("*" if derived[word][1] else "") + derived[word][0]
        return None

    for at, line in enumerate(lines):
        if at in leaving:
            if re.search(r"\b(?:call|invoke) ", line):
                harmless.append(name)
            continue
        line = line.split(";")[0]
        # (Names in quotes too, as Mojo's: "std::builtin::...".)
        named = [word.strip('"') for word in re.findall(
            r'@("[^"]+"|[\w.$]+)', line)
            if word.strip('"') in globals_ and not globals_[word.strip('"')]]
        # (Where a store or a load is to: its address, a global's or one
        # pointing into one; the names of the line where it is no simple
        # value, a constant expression say.)
        address = None
        if re.match(r"\s*store ", line):
            address = line.rsplit(", ptr ", 1)[-1].split()[0] \
                if ", ptr " in line else None
        # (An atomic change whose result is not used has no `%x = `.)
        changes = re.match(r"\s*(?:" + IR_VALUE + r" = )?(atomicrmw|cmpxchg) ",
                           line)
        if changes or re.search(r"= load ", line):
            found = re.search(r"\bptr\s+(\S+)", line.split(" = ", 1)[-1])
            address = found.group(1) if found else None
        simple = address is not None and re.fullmatch(
            "(?:" + IR_GLOBAL + "|" + IR_VALUE + "),?", address)
        if re.match(r"\s*store ", line) or changes:
            if simple:
                writes += [memory(address)] if memory(address) else []
            else:
                writes += named
        elif re.search(r"= load ", line):
            if at in deciding:
                pass
            elif simple:
                reads += [memory(address)] if memory(address) else []
            else:
                reads += named
        elif re.search(r"getelementptr", line):
            reads += named
        if re.search(r"\b(?:call|invoke) [^@%(]*\basm\b", line):
            calls.append("inline assembly")
            continue
        called = re.search(
            r'\b(?:call|invoke) [^@%]*(@"[^"]+"|@[\w.$]+|%[\w.]+)\(', line)
        if not called:
            continue
        callee = called.group(1)
        if callee.startswith("%"):
            calls.append("a function by its address")
            continue
        callee = callee[1:].strip('"')
        # (The pointers into globals' memory it is given: what it writes
        # through them, a memset's or memcpy's first, or a function's that
        # writes what it is given, is written.)
        given = [memory(word) for word in re.findall(
            IR_GLOBAL + "|" + IR_VALUE, line[called.end():])]
        if re.match(r"llvm\.mem(set|cpy|move)", callee):
            if given and given[0]:
                writes.append(given[0])
            reads += [word for word in given[1:2] if word]
            continue
        if callee in functions and memory_of(functions[callee][1],
                                             ("argmem",)) == "writes":
            writes += [word for word in given if word]
        # (What LLVM's intrinsics do, the function's own attributes say.)
        if callee.startswith("llvm.") or callee not in functions \
                or callee in seen:
            continue
        defined, attributes, _ = functions[callee]
        if memory_of(attributes) == "pure":
            continue
        after = lines[at + 1] if at + 1 < len(lines) else ""
        if harmless_call(callee, attributes, after):
            harmless.append(callee)
            continue
        if defined:
            more = why_not_pure(callee, functions, globals_, seen, harmless)
            writes += more[0]
            reads += more[1]
            calls += more[2]
        else:
            calls.append(callee + "()")
    unique = lambda names: list(dict.fromkeys(names))
    # (A global read for the pointer it holds is said by what is written
    # through that.)
    return (unique(writes), [name for name in unique(reads)
                             if name not in writes and "*" + name not in
                             writes], unique(calls))


# (What every process has of its own and alike, which a proc may use on
# every rank: the C library's standard streams, by their names in glibc,
# macOS and Windows' C libraries.)
STANDARD_STREAMS = {"stdout", "stderr", "stdin", "__stdoutp", "__stderrp",
                    "__stdinp", "_IO_2_1_stdout_", "_IO_2_1_stderr_",
                    "_IO_2_1_stdin_"}


def distributed_warning(cname, name, where, language, shown, functions,
                        globals_):
    """Says, at where a `for` over distributed entities calls the extern
    proc `name`, that its code writes or reads global memory: on many ranks
    each partition calls it for its own rows (at once where partitions
    share a process, from threads of their own), and each process has its
    own of what it keeps, which what a proc called outside a `for` sets
    (on rank 0 alone) is not."""
    writes, reads, _ = why_not_pure(cname, functions, globals_)
    writes = [w for w in writes if w.lstrip("*") not in STANDARD_STREAMS]
    reads = [r for r in reads if r.lstrip("*") not in STANDARD_STREAMS]
    if not writes and not reads:
        return
    writes, reads = named_memory(writes), named_memory(reads)
    said = []
    if writes:
        said.append("writes " + ", ".join(writes[:4]) +
                    (" and more" if len(writes) > 4 else ""))
    if reads:
        said.append("reads " + ", ".join(reads[:4]) +
                    (" and more" if len(reads) > 4 else ""))
    sys.stderr.write(
        f"{where or shown}: warning: extern proc '{name}' is called in a "
        f"`for` over distributed entities, and its {language} ({cname} in "
        f"{shown}) {' and '.join(said)}. On many ranks each partition calls "
        "it for its own rows, at once where partitions share a process, and "
        "each process has its own of what it keeps: give it what it needs "
        "as arguments, or call it outside the `for`\n")


def named_memory(names):
    """Globals as why_not_pure gives them, as they are said: g, or "what g
    points to" for *g; demangled."""
    told = demangled([name.lstrip("*") for name in names])
    return [f"what {plain} points to" if name.startswith("*") else plain
            for name, plain in zip(names, told)]


def demangled(names):
    """Rust's and C++'s names as written (core::panicking::panic), by
    LLVM's llvm-cxxfilt where there is one; else as they are."""
    tool = next((path for path in [os.path.join(directory, "llvm-cxxfilt")
                                   for directory in llvm_directories()]
                 + [shutil.which("llvm-cxxfilt") or ""]
                 if os.path.isfile(path) and os.access(path, os.X_OK)), None)
    if not names or tool is None:
        return names
    # (Without what LLVM adds to a name it makes internal: ".0", ".llvm.7".)
    plain = [re.sub(r"(\.llvm)?\.\d+$", "", name) for name in names]
    told = subprocess.run([tool], input="\n".join(plain) + "\n", text=True,
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    lines = told.stdout.splitlines()
    return lines if told.returncode == 0 and len(lines) == len(names) \
        else names


def rust_ir(crate, directory, host, verbose):
    """The LLVM IR of a Rust crate's library (crate: its folder), as cargo
    builds it optimised, in a folder of its own beside the cache (as the
    crate's build.rs is given ENT_INCLUDE, the program's headers); None
    where it cannot be had."""
    cargo = shutil.which("cargo", path=host.get("PATH"))
    if cargo is None:
        if verbose:
            sys.stderr.write(f"{crate}: note: there is no cargo to read its "
                             "Rust to check its extern fns\n")
        return None
    target = os.path.join(cache_directory() + "-rust", hashlib.sha256(
        os.path.realpath(crate).encode()).hexdigest()[:16])
    deps = os.path.join(target, "release", "deps")
    built = subprocess.run(
        [cargo, "rustc", "--release", "--quiet", "--lib", "--manifest-path",
         os.path.join(crate, "Cargo.toml"), "--target-dir", target, "--",
         "--emit=llvm-ir", "-C", "codegen-units=1"],
        env=dict(host, ENT_INCLUDE=os.path.abspath(directory)),
        stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
    # (The library's IR: its name, or the package's, with _ for -; the
    # newest, as cargo keeps those of other flags or versions too.)
    with open(os.path.join(crate, "Cargo.toml")) as manifest:
        text = manifest.read()
    names = re.findall(r'^\s*name\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"', text, re.M)
    lib = re.search(r'\[lib\][^\[]*?^\s*name\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"', text,
                    re.M | re.S)
    name = (lib.group(1) if lib else names[0] if names else "").replace(
        "-", "_")
    made = [os.path.join(deps, entry) for entry in (
        os.listdir(deps) if os.path.isdir(deps) else [])
        if entry.startswith(name + "-") and entry.endswith(".ll")]
    if built.returncode or not name or not made:
        if verbose:
            sys.stderr.write(f"{crate}: note: its Rust could not be read to "
                             "check its extern fns:\n" + built.stderr)
        return None
    return max(made, key=os.path.getmtime)


# (What a module's language is called, by the file next to its .emit.)
LANGUAGES = {".mojo": "Mojo", ".zig": "Zig", ".odin": "Odin",
             ".goose": "Goose", ".rs": "Rust", ".swift": "Swift"}


def emitted_ir(emit, index, clang, cc, cxx, directory, host, verbose):
    """The LLVM IR a module's .emit writes, optimised as C's is (by the
    `opt` of the compiler's LLVM): (its language, the file shown, the IR's
    path); None where it writes none or that cannot be read."""
    with open(emit) as source:
        command = source.read().strip()
    written = os.path.join(directory, "emitted.%d.ll" % index)
    optimised = os.path.join(directory, "emitted.%d.o2.ll" % index)
    environment = dict(host, ENT_OUTPUT=written,
                       ENT_LINK=written + ".link",
                       ENT_INCLUDE=os.path.abspath(directory),
                       ENT_CC=cc, ENT_CXX=cxx, ENT_CLANG=clang,
                       ENT_CPU=" ".join(cpu_flags()))
    said = subprocess.run(command, shell=True, env=environment,
                          cwd=os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(emit)),
                          stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                          text=True)
    opt = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(clang), "opt")
    if not said.returncode and os.path.isfile(written):
        said = subprocess.run([opt if os.path.isfile(opt) else "opt", "-O2",
                               "-S", written, "-o", optimised],
                              stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
    if said.returncode or not os.path.isfile(optimised):
        if verbose:
            sys.stderr.write(f"{emit}: note: its IR could not be had to "
                             "check its extern fns:\n" + said.stderr)
        return None
    stem = os.path.splitext(emit)[0]
    language, shown = "code", emit
    for extension, name in LANGUAGES.items():
        if os.path.isfile(stem + extension):
            language, shown = name, stem + extension
            break
    return language, shown, optimised


def check_externs(clang, cc, cxx, sources, crates, emits, fns, directory,
                  flags, host, verbose, distributed=()):
    """Says, as warnings at their declarations, which extern fns the C of
    the program's modules, or their Rust, shows are no fns: that write or
    read what their arguments do not decide, or call what cannot be seen
    into (unless they are vouched for: ENT_SAME_RESULT in C, a static
    ent_same_result_<name> in any language). Memory had and given back,
    and calls that do not return (an abort, a panic), are no reason. Each
    C file is compiled to LLVM IR by the LLVM of the compiler, optimised
    (inlined, its functions' memory worked out); each Rust crate (the
    folder of a module's .build with a Cargo.toml) by cargo. Those not
    found in either (in an object, say) are said with `verbose`.

    `distributed` (on many ranks): the extern procs a `for` over
    distributed entities calls, (C name, name, where it is called), said
    where their code writes or reads global memory
    (distributed_warning)."""
    if not fns and not distributed:
        return
    left = dict((cname, (name, where)) for cname, name, where in fns)
    procs = dict((cname, (name, where)) for cname, name, where in distributed)
    readings = []
    for index, source in enumerate(sources):
        language = "c++" if source.endswith(".cpp") else "c"
        ir_path = os.path.join(directory, "externs.%d.ll" % index)
        compiled = subprocess.run(
            [clang, "-O2", "-S", "-emit-llvm", "-w", "-x", language,
             "-I", directory, *VENDORED, *flags, source, "-o", ir_path]
            + host_includes(cxx if language == "c++" else cc, language),
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
        if compiled.returncode:
            if verbose:
                sys.stderr.write(f"{source}: note: could not be read to "
                                 "check its extern fns:\n" + compiled.stderr)
            continue
        readings.append(("C", source, ir_path))
    for crate in crates:
        ir_path = rust_ir(crate, directory, host, verbose)
        if ir_path:
            readings.append(("Rust", os.path.join(crate, "Cargo.toml"),
                             ir_path))
    for index, emit in enumerate(emits):
        reading = emitted_ir(emit, index, clang, cc, cxx, directory, host,
                             verbose)
        if reading:
            readings.append(reading)
    for language, source, ir_path in readings:
        with open(ir_path) as read:
            functions, globals_, vouched = ir_functions(read.read())
        vouched |= {name[len("ent_same_result_"):] for name in
                    list(globals_) + [function for function in functions
                                      if functions[function][0]]
                    if name.startswith("ent_same_result_")}
        shown = os.path.relpath(source) if not os.path.relpath(
            source).startswith("../..") else source
        for cname in [cname for cname in left if cname in functions
                      and functions[cname][0]]:
            name, where = left.pop(cname)
            kind = memory_of(functions[cname][1])
            if kind == "pure" or cname in vouched:
                continue
            harmless = []
            writes, reads, calls = why_not_pure(cname, functions, globals_,
                                                harmless=harmless)
            # (Only memory had and given back, or a way out, and what the
            # attributes say of the rest is no more than that.)
            if harmless and not (writes or reads or calls) and memory_of(
                    functions[cname][1], ("argmem", "other", "target_mem0",
                                          "target_mem1")) in ("pure",
                                                              "unknown"):
                continue
            # (Where the attributes say it writes, and all it writes is an
            # array of its own: LLVM did not see that its stores through a
            # `phi` of its allocas stay there.)
            if kind == "writes" and not (writes or reads or calls) and \
                    writes_only_own(functions[cname][2] or []):
                continue
            # (What it writes, where its attributes say nothing, as where it
            # writes through a global's pointer and has a way out.)
            if writes:
                kind = "writes"
            writes, reads = named_memory(writes), named_memory(reads)
            called = [call[:-2] for call in calls if call.endswith("()")]
            written = dict(zip(called, demangled(called)))
            calls = [written[call[:-2]] + "()" if call.endswith("()")
                     else call for call in calls]
            said = []
            if writes:
                said.append("writes " + ", ".join(writes[:4]) + (
                    " and more" if len(writes) > 4 else ""))
            if reads:
                said.append("reads " + ", ".join(reads[:4]) + (
                    " and more" if len(reads) > 4 else ""))
            if calls:
                said.append("calls " + ", ".join(calls[:4]) + (
                    " and %d more" % (len(calls) - 4) if len(calls) > 4
                    else ""))
            if not said:
                said.append({"hidden": "uses memory no IR names (malloc's, "
                             "a library's state)",
                             "writes": "writes memory other than its own",
                             "reads": "reads memory other than its "
                             "arguments"}.get(kind, "does what cannot be "
                                              "seen into"))
            vouch = {"C": "mark its C ENT_SAME_RESULT",
                     "Rust": f"vouch for it with `#[no_mangle] pub static "
                     f"ent_same_result_{cname}: u8 = 0;`",
                     "Mojo": f"vouch for it with `@export(\"ent_same_result_"
                     f"{cname}\") def ...() abi(\"C\"): pass`"}.get(
                language, f"vouch for it with a static or function "
                f"ent_same_result_{cname} it exports")
            if kind == "writes":
                what, advice = "is no fn", (
                    "A fn may be called at another time, fewer times or on "
                    "many threads at once: declare it 'extern proc'")
            elif kind == "reads":
                what, advice = "may give another result for the same " \
                    "arguments", (
                    "A fn may be called at another time or fewer times: "
                    f"declare it 'extern proc', or {vouch} where what it "
                    "reads does not change once it is called")
            else:
                what, advice = "cannot be checked", (
                    f"{vouch[0].upper()}{vouch[1:]} where its result depends "
                    "on its arguments alone, else declare it 'extern proc'")
            sys.stderr.write(
                f"{where or shown}: warning: extern fn '{name}' {what}: its "
                f"{language} ({cname} in {shown}) {' and '.join(said)}. "
                f"{advice}\n")
        for cname in [cname for cname in procs if cname in functions
                      and functions[cname][0]]:
            name, where = procs.pop(cname)
            if cname not in vouched:
                distributed_warning(cname, name, where, language, shown,
                                    functions, globals_)
    if verbose:
        for cname, (name, where) in left.items():
            sys.stderr.write(f"{where}: note: extern fn '{name}' is "
                             "checked only as it runs: it is defined outside "
                             "the .c and .cpp files, Rust crates and modules "
                             "with a .emit of the build\n")


def build(arguments, output, directory):
    own = [os.environ.get("ENT_BIN"), os.path.join(ROOT, "build/bin")]
    own = [path for path in own if path]
    translate = find_tool("ent-translate", own)
    optimise = find_tool("ent-opt", own)
    clang = find_tool("clang", llvm_directories())

    include_directories = arguments.I + [os.path.join(ROOT, "devices")]
    program = arguments.program
    stem = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(program))[0]
    # With --capacity, the program as it is with `capacity Name N` after
    # it, which holds over what it and its modules said; read from here,
    # its modules found where it is.
    if arguments.capacity:
        with open(program) as source:
            text = source.read()
        program = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".ent")
        with open(program, "w") as out:
            out.write(text + "\n// (ent build --capacity)\n" + "".join(
                f"capacity {name} {rows}\n"
                for name, rows in capacities(arguments)))
        include_directories = ([os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(
            arguments.program))] + include_directories)
    includes = [flag for path in include_directories for flag in ("-I", path)]

    module = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".mlir")
    # (With where each op comes from in the .ent, which the lines of the
    # program's machine code are then of.)
    # (On many threads its indexes are made so.)
    imported = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".imported.mlir")
    run([translate, "--import-ent", program, "--mlir-print-debuginfo",
         "-o", imported] + (["--parallel"] if arguments.parallel else [])
        + includes)
    # (The indexes that would serve its `for`s, which it does not declare:
    # each said, at the `for`s; with --infer-indexes made, as if declared.)
    if arguments.infer_indexes or not arguments.no_index_remarks:
        found = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".indexes.ent")
        # (Of a trace: only `for`s whose loops took time there.)
        times = ""
        if arguments.tune:
            times = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".times.json")
            loop_times(arguments.tune, times)
            times = " times=" + times
        run([optimise, imported, "--ent-infer-indexes=out=" + found + times
             + (" remarks=0" if arguments.no_index_remarks else ""),
             "--mlir-print-op-on-diagnostic=false", "-o", os.devnull])
        if arguments.infer_indexes and os.path.getsize(found):
            run([translate, "--import-ent", program, "--inferred=" + found,
                 "--mlir-print-debuginfo", "-o", imported]
                + (["--parallel"] if arguments.parallel else []) + includes)
    # (Each index made again before what reads it, where what it is made
    # of changed: before the systems are staged and lowered; where built
    # for a GPU, an index may be made there: EntIndexes.) Before the
    # world's header is written: what the indexes add and change is part
    # of the world as the program has it, which the header must say as
    # the lowering lays it out.
    choices = []
    if arguments.gpu and not os.environ.get("ENT_INDEXES_ON_CPU"):
        choices.append("gpu=1")
    if arguments.ranks:
        choices.append("ranks=1")
    if arguments.no_index_remarks:
        choices.append("remarks=0")
    indexes = "--ent-indexes" + ("=" + " ".join(choices) if choices else "")
    run([optimise, imported, indexes, "--mlir-print-debuginfo",
         "--mlir-print-op-on-diagnostic=false", "-o", module])
    header = os.path.join(directory, "ent_world.h")
    run([translate, "--ent-to-c-header", module, "-o", header])
    # (A web page's memory is at most 4 GB, the world and all else: one
    # made for a machine may be larger, of capacities it never uses.)
    if arguments.web:
        with open(header) as source:
            size = re.search(r"#define ENT_WORLD_BYTES (\d+)ull", source.read())
        if size and int(size.group(1)) > WEB_WORLD_MOST:
            sys.exit(f"ent: --web: the world is {int(size.group(1)) / 2**30:.1f}"
                     f" GB, and a web page's memory at most 4 GB: give its "
                     "largest components or buffers smaller capacities "
                     "(--capacity Name=N)")
    # What the C of extern fns and procs gets: their declarations, without
    # the world.
    run([translate, "--ent-to-c-extern-header", module, "-o",
         os.path.join(directory, "ent_extern.h")])
    # Also under the program's name, for C written for this program only.
    shutil.copyfile(header, os.path.join(directory, stem + "_world.h"))

    lowering = "--ent-lower-to-loops"
    gpu = " gpu=1" if arguments.gpu else ""
    # (Choices of a trace, see tuned_loops.)
    tuning = ""
    if arguments.tune:
        choices = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".tune.json")
        with open(choices, "w") as out:
            json.dump(tuned_loops(arguments.tune), out, indent=1)
        tuning = " tune=" + choices
    # (On many ranks: devices/ranks.c, and MPI's own flags.)
    ranks = " ranks=1" if arguments.ranks else ""
    if arguments.windows and (arguments.gpu or arguments.ranks):
        sys.exit("ent: --windows: not with --gpu or --ranks")
    if arguments.ranks and arguments.partitions:
        ranks += " partitions=%d" % arguments.ranks
    # (The indexes are made already, above.)
    passes = [lowering + "=fuse-systems=1" + gpu + ranks + tuning,
              "--symbol-dce"]
    # (Kernels are made of the GPU's loops before what is left of them is
    # the CPU's; and the columns are read by their addresses before, so
    # that a kernel is given those.)
    before, after, finish = gpu_passes(gpu_chip()) if gpu else ([], [], [])
    # (The kernels' math, from CUDA's libdevice, which the passes making
    # them into PTX find by CUDA_PATH: where it is not said, where CUDA's
    # header is looked for too. Without it a kernel that calls one fails
    # to load.)
    if gpu:
        os.environ.setdefault("CUDA_PATH", "/opt/cuda")
    # (ENT_TBAA=1: the loads and stores of the world tell LLVM which part
    # of it they are in, a count or the rows; not yet chosen for all, see
    # bench/RESULTS.md.)
    # (ENT_LOOP_CHECKS=0: each append checked and each read guarded, as
    # before --ent-loop-checks; to compare.)
    checks = ([] if os.environ.get("ENT_LOOP_CHECKS") == "0"
              else ["--ent-loop-checks"])
    views = checks + ["--cse", "--ent-lower-views" + (
        "=tbaa=1" if os.environ.get("ENT_TBAA") == "1" else "")]
    if gpu:
        passes += views + before
    link = []
    if arguments.parallel:
        # (ENT_PARALLEL_MIN: from how many entities, values sent or events
        # a loop runs on many threads; for tests, which have few.)
        least = os.environ.get("ENT_PARALLEL_MIN", "")
        least = (" parallel-min-entities=%d parallel-min-events=%d"
                 % (int(least), int(least))) if least else ""
        # (ENT_ATOMIC_SENDS=1: what is sent combined atomically where it
        # is sent, to measure what the order costs; not the same bytes.)
        if os.environ.get("ENT_ATOMIC_SENDS") == "1":
            least += " atomic-sends=1"
        passes = ["--ent-schedule",
                  lowering + "=fuse-systems=1 parallel-entities=1 "
                  "parallel-stages=1" + least + gpu + ranks + tuning,
                  "--symbol-dce"] + (views + before if gpu else []) + [
                  "--convert-scf-to-openmp", "--canonicalize",
                  "--ent-omp-nowait"]
        # The OpenMP runtime of the LLVM that compiled the program, by its
        # file: its directory is not one to look for other libraries in.
        library = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(clang)), "lib")
        runtime = "libomp.dylib" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "libomp.so"
        link = [os.path.join(library, runtime), "-Wl,-rpath," + library]
        # (For Windows, MinGW's: llvm-mingw has one.)
        if arguments.windows:
            link = ["-lomp"]

    files, assets = made_of(arguments)
    given = [os.path.splitext(path)[0] + extension for path in files
             for extension in IMPLEMENTATIONS
             if os.path.isfile(os.path.splitext(path)[0] + extension)]
    # (The C of the modules' extern fns, which is checked; the C below
    # that comes with the compiler has none.)
    implementing = [path for path in given + arguments.sources
                    if path.endswith((".c", ".cpp"))]
    # (And the Rust: a module built by a command, next to a Cargo.toml.)
    crates = list(dict.fromkeys(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)) for path in given
        if path.endswith(".build") and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(
            os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)), "Cargo.toml"))))
    # What a `for` sends from a loop of its body (on many threads) and
    # the edges it connects there are kept by C that comes with the
    # compiler.
    given.append(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "sends.c"))
    # And where the world lives (in a file, with ENT_WORLD_DIR).
    given.append(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "memory.c"))
    # (And what keeps a relation's edges in order where few change.)
    given.append(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "edges.c"))
    # (With --trace: what keeps the times, see the ent-trace pass.)
    if arguments.trace:
        given.append(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "trace.c"))
    if arguments.gpu:
        given.append(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "gpu.c"))
        link.append("-lcuda")
    if arguments.ranks:
        given.append(os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "ranks.c"))
        link += mpi_flags()[1]
    for path in arguments.sources:
        if os.path.splitext(path)[1] not in (".c", ".cpp", ".o", ".a"):
            sys.exit(f"ent: do not know how to build '{path}' (expected "
                     ".c, .cpp, .o or .a)")
    given += arguments.sources
    # The libraries the modules ask for, after everything that uses them.
    # (For a web page a module's .web.link where it has one; $NAMES in it
    # are the environment's, and ENT_RAYLIB_WEB is raylib_web().)
    for path in files:
        flags = os.path.splitext(path)[0] + ".link"
        if arguments.web:
            flags = os.path.splitext(path)[0] + ".web.link"
        if os.path.isfile(flags):
            with open(flags) as source:
                said = source.read()
            if arguments.web:
                said = os.path.expandvars(said.replace(
                    "$ENT_RAYLIB_WEB", raylib_web()))
            link = shlex.split(said, comments=True) + link
    # (A page with a window has a font in it: there are none of the
    # machine's on the web.)
    if arguments.web and any(
            os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(path))[0] == "window"
            and os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)) ==
            os.path.join(ROOT, "devices") for path in files):
        font = web_font()
        if font:
            link += ["--preload-file",
                     f"{os.path.realpath(font)}@/ent/plain.ttf"]
    # (And what writes the IR of a module in another language, Mojo's.)
    emits = [os.path.splitext(path)[0] + ".emit" for path in given
             if path.endswith(".build") and os.path.isfile(
                 os.path.splitext(path)[0] + ".emit")]

    # The program by the LLVM that made its IR; the rest, and the link, by
    # the machine's compiler. Each to an object, then one link.
    cc, cxx = c_compilers(arguments)
    # It runs as on its own: an LLVM on the PATH would lend it its linker,
    # and with that its idea of where libraries are.
    toolchain = [os.path.realpath(path) for path in llvm_directories()]
    host = dict(os.environ)
    if os.path.realpath(os.path.dirname(cc)) not in toolchain:
        host["PATH"] = os.pathsep.join(
            path for path in os.environ.get("PATH", "").split(os.pathsep)
            if os.path.realpath(path) not in toolchain)
    # On a Mac the machine's compiler does not know Homebrew's libraries
    # (raylib, say), as one on Linux knows those in /usr: they are looked
    # for there after where the environment says.
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        for variable, folder in (("CPATH", "include"),
                                 ("LIBRARY_PATH", "lib")):
            more = [os.path.join(prefix, folder) for prefix in homebrew()]
            more = [path for path in more if os.path.isdir(path)]
            host[variable] = os.pathsep.join(
                [path for path in host.get(variable, "").split(os.pathsep)
                 if path] + more)
    others = []
    for index, path in enumerate(given):
        name, extension = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(path))
        built = os.path.join(directory, f"{name}.{index}.o")
        if extension in (".o", ".a"):
            others.append(path)
            continue
        if extension == ".c":
            cuda = []
            if gpu:
                cuda = ["-DENT_GPU", "-I", os.path.join(
                    os.environ.get("CUDA_PATH", "/opt/cuda"), "include")]
            mpi = mpi_flags()[0] if arguments.ranks else []
            # (Never a multiply and an add made one FMA, as in ent's own
            # code: a device's results are then the same on a processor
            # that has FMA and one that has not.)
            web = ["-I", raylib_web()] if arguments.web else []
            run([cc, "-O2", "-ffp-contract=off", *cpu_flags(), "-I", directory,
                 *VENDORED, *cuda, *web,
                 *mpi, "-c", path, "-o", built], env=host)
        elif extension == ".cpp":
            run([cxx, "-O2", "-ffp-contract=off", *cpu_flags(), "-I", directory,
                 *VENDORED, "-c",
                 path, "-o", built], env=host)
        else:
            with open(path) as source:
                command = source.read().strip()
            # (And where it may write the linker's arguments for what it
            # needs, known only as it builds: where a compiler's own
            # libraries are, say.)
            linking = built + ".link"
            environment = dict(host, ENT_OUTPUT=built, ENT_LINK=linking,
                               ENT_INCLUDE=os.path.abspath(directory),
                               ENT_CC=cc, ENT_CXX=cxx,
                               ENT_CPU=" ".join(cpu_flags()))
            for old in (built, linking):
                if os.path.exists(old):
                    os.remove(old)
            run(command, shell=True, env=environment,
                cwd=os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)))
            if not os.path.isfile(built):
                sys.exit(f"ent: '{path}' did not write $ENT_OUTPUT")
            if os.path.isfile(linking):
                with open(linking) as source:
                    link = shlex.split(source.read(), comments=True) + link
        others.append(built)
    # Whether its extern fns are fns: what their C shows (not of a program
    # for Windows, whose headers are not this machine's), and with
    # --check-externs, as it runs (the C that checks, below).
    checks = os.path.join(directory, "ent_checks.c")
    run([translate, "--ent-to-c-extern-checks", module, "-o", checks])
    fns, distributed = [], []
    with open(checks) as source:
        for line in source:
            found = re.match(r"// (fn|distributed) (\S+) (\S+) ?(.*)$", line)
            if found:
                (fns if found.group(1) == "fn" else distributed).append(
                    found.groups()[1:])
            elif not line.startswith("//"):
                break
    if not arguments.windows and not arguments.web:
        extra = []
        if gpu:
            extra = ["-DENT_GPU", "-I", os.path.join(
                os.environ.get("CUDA_PATH", "/opt/cuda"), "include")]
        if arguments.ranks:
            extra += mpi_flags()[0]
        check_externs(clang, cc, cxx, implementing, crates, emits, fns,
                      directory, extra, host, arguments.check_externs,
                      distributed if arguments.ranks else ())
    # What of the program that C calls, which keeps its name; the rest
    # of it is the program's own (internal), which LLVM inlines where it
    # is called from one place, or where that pays, and drops where it is
    # not called at all.
    keep = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".keep")
    used = set()
    if others:
        names = find_tool("llvm-nm", llvm_directories())
        listed = run([names, "--undefined-only", "--just-symbol-name"] + others,
                     stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True).stdout
        for name in listed.split():
            if name.endswith(":"):
                continue  # (the file the names after it are of)
            if sys.platform == "darwin" and name.startswith("_"):
                name = name[1:]
            used.add(name)
    with open(keep, "w") as names:
        names.write("".join(name + "\n" for name in sorted(used)))

    # To LLVM bitcode in the same run: not written out as text for another
    # tool to read again.
    llvm = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".bc")
    cflags = shlex.split(os.environ.get("ENT_CFLAGS", ""))
    if arguments.windows:
        cflags.append("--target=" + WINDOWS)
    if arguments.web:
        cflags.append("--target=" + WEB)
    features = machine_features(clang, cflags)
    run([optimise, module] + passes +
        # (What the lowering wrote more than once, once, and the columns
        # read and written by their addresses: less to turn into LLVM's
        # IR, and for LLVM to read.)
        ([] if gpu else views) + after +
        (["--ent-trace"] if arguments.trace else []) +
        # (A failed check says why on stderr: see --ent-stops.)
        ["--ent-stops", "--convert-scf-to-cf", "--convert-to-llvm"] + finish +
        ["--reconcile-unrealized-casts",
         # (Each instruction with the line of the .ent it was made from,
         # for a profiler or a debugger to say: line tables only.)
         "--ensure-debug-info-scope-on-llvm-func",
         "--ent-emit-llvm=output=" + llvm + " keep=" + keep +
         (" features=" + features if features else "") +
         (" pointer-bits=32" if arguments.web else ""),
         "-o", os.devnull])

    flags = ["-O2", "-ffp-contract=off", *cflags, "-Wno-override-module", "-c"]
    if arguments.instrument:
        flags.append("-fprofile-generate=" + instrumented(arguments))
        # Its library, linked by the machine's compiler: kept in by the
        # symbol that LLVM's own driver would ask for, which starts it.
        apple = sys.platform == "darwin"
        link += [profile_runtime(clang),
                 "-Wl,-u," + ("_" if apple else "") + "__llvm_profile_runtime"]
    if arguments.profile:
        merged = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".profdata")
        run([find_tool("llvm-profdata", llvm_directories()), "merge",
             "-o", merged] + profiles(arguments.profile))
        flags.append("-fprofile-use=" + merged)
    pieces = piece_count(llvm)
    split = shutil.which("llvm-split", path=os.path.dirname(clang))
    if pieces > 1 and split:
        # A big program is optimised whole, and then made into machine
        # code in pieces, each on a core of its own: its functions shared
        # out, those of one piece that another calls known to both.
        # (With ENT_SPLIT=first it is shared out before it is optimised:
        # built in two thirds of the time, and a tenth slower to run,
        # since no piece can take in what another has.)
        first = os.environ.get("ENT_SPLIT") == "first"
        whole = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".opt.bc")
        if first:
            whole = llvm
        else:
            run([clang, *flags, "-emit-llvm", llvm, "-o", whole])
        prefix = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".piece")
        run([split, f"-j{pieces}", whole, "-o", prefix])
        objects = [f"{prefix}{index}.o" for index in range(pieces)]
        compilers = [subprocess.Popen(
            [clang, *flags] +
            ([] if first else ["-Xclang", "-disable-llvm-passes"]) +
            ["-x", "ir", f"{prefix}{index}", "-o", built])
            for index, built in enumerate(objects)]
        failed = [compiler.wait() for compiler in compilers]
        if any(failed):
            sys.exit(max(failed))
    else:
        objects = [os.path.join(directory, stem + ".o")]
        run([clang, *flags, llvm, "-o", objects[0]])
    if arguments.check_externs and fns:
        # The program calls each extern fn's check in its place, which
        # calls it.
        apple = sys.platform == "darwin" and not arguments.windows
        mark = "_" if apple else ""
        renames = [flag for cname, _, _ in fns for flag in (
            "--redefine-sym", f"{mark}{cname}={mark}ent__checked_{cname}")]
        rename = find_tool("llvm-objcopy", llvm_directories())
        for built in objects:
            run([rename] + renames + [built])
        objects.append(os.path.join(directory, "ent_checks.o"))
        run([cc, "-O1", "-I", directory, "-c", checks, "-o", objects[-1]],
            env=host)
    objects += others
    # Where the program's source and its modules' are, written into it:
    # the files they name are looked for there last (devices/ent_files.h).
    # (Not into one for Windows: they are this machine's.)
    folders = []
    for path in [] if arguments.windows or arguments.web else files:
        if os.path.dirname(path) not in folders:
            folders.append(os.path.dirname(path))
    written = os.path.join(directory, "ent_folders.c")
    with open(written, "w") as source:
        source.write("const char ent_program_folders[] = %s;\n"
                     % json.dumps(os.pathsep.join(folders)))
        # With --embed, also the files it declares, by the names it
        # knows them by. Their bytes the assembler takes from the files
        # as they are, however many they are.
        held = (list(assets.items()) if arguments.embed and not arguments.web
                else [])
        if held:
            for index in range(len(held)):
                source.write("extern const unsigned char "
                             f"ent_asset_{index}[];\n")
            source.write("const struct { const char *name; const unsigned "
                         "char *bytes; unsigned long size; } "
                         "ent_program_assets[] = {\n")
            for index, (name, where) in enumerate(held):
                source.write(f"  {{{json.dumps(name)}, ent_asset_{index}, "
                             f"{os.path.getsize(where)}}},\n")
            source.write("  {0, 0, 0}};\n")
        if arguments.parallel:
            # A thread without work waits five milliseconds for more,
            # then sleeps: awake through the short stretches a program
            # does on one core between its parallel loops (one woken
            # takes up to a millisecond to start), asleep through long
            # ones. (The OpenMP runtime would have it spin for a fifth of
            # a second: through all a frame does on one core, drawing
            # say, which the spinning cores slow down; see
            # bench/RESULTS.md, 2026-10-09 and 2026-10-21.) On a Mac (and
            # on Windows, which is not measured yet) it sleeps at once: threads cannot be bound there, and with a
            # thread for each of an M4 Max's 16 cores, waiting so, the
            # landing check took 124 s where one core takes 63 and 12
            # threads that sleep 37. And so does a program with a window
            # (devices/window.ent), anywhere: it does a frame's work and
            # waits for the screen, and its parallel loops come less than
            # a millisecond apart, so threads that wait never sleep:
            # Pixalorien on a Ryzen AI 9 HX 370 (24 hardware threads) at
            # its title, 20 s, 247 s of the CPUs waiting 5 ms (149 waiting
            # one), 53 sleeping at once; its landing check 22.3 and 20.8 s
            # (447 and 163 s of the CPUs); a world run as fast as it goes
            # (its strategies check, no drawing) 80.9 and 91.3 s.
            #
            # And there is one thread for each CPU the program may run on
            # (each hardware thread of a core), bound to it, but for the
            # others of the core of the first, which runs the program
            # alone between the loops: a thread that waits for the next
            # loop on the other half of that core takes half of it.
            # Against one thread a core, the LJ melt 0.198 -> 0.176 s,
            # the epidemic 11.0 -> 9.3 ms a day, the traffic highway
            # 1.343 -> 1.193 s, beecs's 100 colonies 2.13 -> 2.22 s (it
            # is mostly on one core); with a thread on the other half of
            # the first core too, beecs 2.9 s (bench/RESULTS.md,
            # 2026-10-22). Not on many ranks, whose processes or
            # partitions would each bind theirs to the same CPUs. What
            # the environment says holds over all of this.
            bind = "0" if arguments.ranks else "1"
            # (A program whose main thread waits on the world outside:
            # the window module (devices/window.ent, or one standing in
            # for it, as the tests' window without a window), or the http
            # device, whose frames are a poll of a few milliseconds:
            # threads waiting five for the next loop would never sleep,
            # and spend a core each for nothing.)
            windowed = any(os.path.basename(path) in ("window.ent", "http.ent")
                           for path in made_of(arguments)[0])
            wait = "0" if windowed else "5"
            source.write(
                "#define _GNU_SOURCE\n"
                "#include <stdio.h>\n"
                "#include <stdlib.h>\n"
                "#include <string.h>\n"
                "#ifdef __linux__\n"
                "#include <sched.h>\n"
                "#endif\n"
                "#ifdef __APPLE__\n"
                "#include <sys/sysctl.h>\n"
                "#endif\n"
                "#ifdef _WIN32\n"
                "// (Windows's C library has no setenv; what it puts is\n"
                "// in the process's environment, where OpenMP reads.)\n"
                "static int setenv(const char *name, const char *value,\n"
                "                  int replace) {\n"
                "  if (!replace && getenv(name)) return 0;\n"
                "  return _putenv_s(name, value);\n"
                "}\n"
                "#endif\n"
                "#ifdef __linux__\n"
                "// Whether `cpu` is listed in a sysfs list (\"0,12\", \"0-1\").\n"
                "static int ent_listed(const char *path, int cpu) {\n"
                "  FILE *list = fopen(path, \"r\");\n"
                "  if (!list) return 0;\n"
                "  int low, high, found = 0;\n"
                "  char after = 0;\n"
                "  while (fscanf(list, \"%d\", &low) == 1) {\n"
                "    high = low;\n"
                "    if (fscanf(list, \"%c\", &after) == 1 && after == '-') {\n"
                "      if (fscanf(list, \"%d\", &high) != 1) break;\n"
                "      if (fscanf(list, \"%c\", &after) != 1) after = 0;\n"
                "    }\n"
                "    found |= low <= cpu && cpu <= high;\n"
                "    if (after != ',') break;\n"
                "  }\n"
                "  fclose(list);\n"
                "  return found;\n"
                "}\n"
                "#endif\n"
                "// The places for the threads, one CPU each (\"{0},{1},..\"):\n"
                "// those the program may run on, but the others of the\n"
                "// first one's core; how many (0 where that is not known).\n"
                "static int ent_places(char *places, size_t room) {\n"
                "  places[0] = 0;\n"
                "#if defined(__linux__)\n"
                "  cpu_set_t mask;\n"
                "  if (sched_getaffinity(0, sizeof mask, &mask) != 0) return 0;\n"
                "  int first = -1, count = 0;\n"
                "  char path[96];\n"
                "  for (int cpu = 0; cpu < CPU_SETSIZE; cpu++) {\n"
                "    if (!CPU_ISSET(cpu, &mask)) continue;\n"
                "    if (first < 0) {\n"
                "      first = cpu;\n"
                "      snprintf(path, sizeof path, \"/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu%d/\"\n"
                "               \"topology/thread_siblings_list\", cpu);\n"
                "    } else if (ent_listed(path, cpu)) {\n"
                "      continue;\n"
                "    }\n"
                "    size_t at = strlen(places);\n"
                "    if (at + 16 >= room) return 0;\n"
                "    snprintf(places + at, room - at, \"%s{%d}\", count ? \",\" : \"\", cpu);\n"
                "    count++;\n"
                "  }\n"
                "  return count;\n"
                "#elif defined(__APPLE__)\n"
                "  // The performance cores (a Mac of one kind of core has\n"
                "  // no such count).\n"
                "  int cores = 0;\n"
                "  size_t size = sizeof cores;\n"
                "  if (sysctlbyname(\"hw.perflevel0.physicalcpu\", &cores, &size, 0, 0)\n"
                "      == 0 && cores > 0) return cores;\n"
                "  size = sizeof cores;\n"
                "  return sysctlbyname(\"hw.physicalcpu\", &cores, &size, 0, 0) == 0\n"
                "    ? cores : 0;\n"
                "#else\n"
                "  return 0;\n"
                "#endif\n"
                "}\n"
                "__attribute__((constructor)) static void "
                "ent_threads_wait(void) {\n"
                "#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(_WIN32)\n"
                '  if (!getenv("OMP_WAIT_POLICY")) '
                'setenv("KMP_BLOCKTIME", "0", 0);\n'
                "#else\n"
                '  if (!getenv("OMP_WAIT_POLICY")) '
                f'setenv("KMP_BLOCKTIME", "{wait}", 0);\n'
                "#endif\n"
                f"  if (!{bind} || getenv(\"OMP_NUM_THREADS\") || "
                'getenv("OMP_PLACES") ||\n'
                '      getenv("OMP_PROC_BIND") || getenv("KMP_AFFINITY")) return;\n'
                "  static char places[16384];\n"
                "  int count = ent_places(places, sizeof places);\n"
                "  if (count <= 0) return;\n"
                "  char threads[16];\n"
                '  snprintf(threads, sizeof threads, "%d", count);\n'
                '  setenv("OMP_NUM_THREADS", threads, 0);\n'
                "#ifdef __linux__\n"
                '  setenv("OMP_PLACES", places, 0);\n'
                '  setenv("OMP_PROC_BIND", "close", 0);\n'
                "#endif\n"
                "}\n")
    if held:
        apple = sys.platform == "darwin" and not arguments.windows
        bytes_of = os.path.join(directory, "ent_assets.s")
        with open(bytes_of, "w") as source:
            source.write("  .section __TEXT,__const\n" if apple
                         else '  .section .rdata,"dr"\n' if arguments.windows
                         else '  .section .rodata\n')
            for index, (name, where) in enumerate(held):
                label = ("_" if apple else "") + f"ent_asset_{index}"
                source.write(f"  .globl {label}\n  .p2align 4\n{label}:\n"
                             f"  .incbin {json.dumps(os.path.abspath(where))}"
                             "\n  .byte 0\n")
            if not apple and not arguments.windows:
                source.write('  .section .note.GNU-stack,"",%progbits\n')
        objects.append(os.path.join(directory, "ent_assets.o"))
        run([cc, "-c", bytes_of, "-o", objects[-1]], env=host)
    objects.append(os.path.join(directory, "ent_folders.o"))
    run([cc, "-c", written, "-o", objects[-1]], env=host)
    # C++ brings its library; other languages' objects say what they need
    # in their module's .link.
    uses_cxx = any(path.endswith(".cpp") for path in given)
    # (The C maths library, which a fraction's `%` calls, fmod: in libc on
    # macOS.)
    maths = [] if sys.platform == "darwin" else ["-lm"]
    # (devices/text.c asks for OpenMP's omp_in_parallel only where it is
    # there: a weak symbol, which macOS's linker takes only when told it
    # may be missing.)
    if sys.platform == "darwin" and not arguments.windows:
        maths += ["-Wl,-U,_omp_in_parallel"]
    if arguments.web:
        # A page: the program (output.wasm), what starts it (output.js)
        # and the page (output.html) with a canvas, which the window
        # draws on. The program's `main` never gives the browser its turn
        # back (`loop { .. }`): Asyncify lets it wait for the next frame
        # where the window waits for one (raylib's WindowShouldClose).
        # Its files are in the page's own (by their names, as --embed),
        # its world as its capacities say, up to WebAssembly's 4 GB.
        # (-O2: the WebAssembly made smaller and faster as a whole, by
        # Binaryen, and the locals of a function the program's code
        # left unoptimised (what makes the world) shared out: browsers
        # take no function of more than 50,000.)
        # (What the program's functions hold where it waits for a frame
        # kept in ASYNCIFY_STACK_SIZE: 4 KB by default, too few for the
        # layout's drawing, examples/layout_demo.ent.)
        maths += ["-O2", "-sASYNCIFY", "-sASYNCIFY_STACK_SIZE=1MB",
                  "-sALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=1",
                  "-sMAXIMUM_MEMORY=4GB", "-sSTACK_SIZE=8MB",
                  "-sEXIT_RUNTIME=1"]
        # (ENT_WEB_DEBUG=1: the functions' names kept and Emscripten's
        # own checks on, for where a page stops to say where and why.)
        if os.environ.get("ENT_WEB_DEBUG") == "1":
            maths += ["--profiling-funcs", "-sASSERTIONS=1"]
        for name, where in assets.items():
            maths += ["--preload-file", f"{os.path.abspath(where)}@/{name}"]
        shell = os.path.join(ROOT, "devices", "web.html")
        # (With the keys a touch screen is given, where there are: the
        # page with them written in.)
        if arguments.touch_keys:
            with open(shell) as source:
                page = source.read()
            page = page.replace("var entTouchKeys = [];",
                                "var entTouchKeys = "
                                f"{json.dumps(arguments.touch_keys)};")
            shell = os.path.join(directory, "web.html")
            with open(shell, "w") as written_page:
                written_page.write(page)
        if output.endswith(".html"):
            maths += ["--shell-file", shell]
    run([cxx if uses_cxx else cc] + objects + link + maths + ["-o", output],
        env=host)


def launch(arguments, executable):
    """How to start the program: on N ranks through mpirun; for Windows
    with Wine."""
    if arguments.windows:
        wine = shutil.which("wine") or sys.exit("ent: --windows: no wine "
                                                "on the PATH to run it")
        return [wine, executable]
    if not arguments.ranks:
        return [executable]
    mpirun = shutil.which("mpirun")
    if not mpirun:
        sys.exit("ent: --ranks: no mpirun on the PATH")
    return [mpirun, "-n", str(arguments.ranks), "--oversubscribe", executable]


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="ent", description=__doc__,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    parser.add_argument("command", choices=["build", "run"])
    parser.add_argument("program", help="the .ent file with `main`")
    parser.add_argument("sources", nargs="*", metavar="more.c",
                        help="files (.c, .cpp, .o, .a) with the program's "
                        "own extern fns, procs and systems")
    parser.add_argument("-o", dest="output", help="the executable to write "
                        "(build; default: the program's name)")
    parser.add_argument("-I", action="append", default=[], metavar="dir",
                        help="a directory to find imported modules in")
    parser.add_argument("--parallel", action="store_true",
                        help="stage and fuse systems and run them on all "
                        "cores (OpenMP)")
    parser.add_argument("--gpu", action="store_true",
                        help="run the `for`s of `gpu system`s on the "
                        "machine's NVIDIA GPU")
    parser.add_argument("--ranks", type=int, metavar="N",
                        help="build for many ranks (MPI); `run` starts N")
    parser.add_argument("--partitions", action="store_true",
                        help="with --ranks: place entities where they are "
                        "spawned, a partition for each rank")
    parser.add_argument("--embed", action="store_true",
                        help="put the files the program declares (`asset`) "
                        "into the program itself, not next to it")
    parser.add_argument("--instrument", action="store_true",
                        help="count where the program goes when it runs, "
                        "into program.profile (for --profile)")
    parser.add_argument("--profile", metavar="path",
                        help="optimise by the counts of runs of the program "
                        "built with --instrument: their folder or a "
                        ".profraw or .profdata file")
    parser.add_argument("--trace", action="store_true",
                        help="time systems, sorts and parallel loops, said "
                        "on stderr and written to program.trace.json")
    parser.add_argument("--tune", metavar="trace",
                        help="choose for each loop over entities whether it "
                        "runs on many threads, and how it shares its pieces "
                        "out, by how it ran in a trace of the program "
                        "(program.trace.json of a build with --trace)")
    parser.add_argument("--infer-indexes", action="store_true",
                        help="make the indexes the compiler finds would "
                        "serve the program's `for`s, as if it declared "
                        "them (each said in a remark either way)")
    parser.add_argument("--no-index-remarks", action="store_true",
                        help="do not say which indexes would serve the "
                        "program's `for`s")
    parser.add_argument("--windows", action="store_true",
                        help="build a program for 64-bit Windows (an .exe), "
                        "with llvm-mingw's x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang; `run` "
                        "starts it with Wine")
    parser.add_argument("--web", action="store_true",
                        help="build a program for a web page (name.html, "
                        ".js and .wasm), on one thread, with Emscripten's "
                        "emcc; open the page through a web server")
    parser.add_argument("--capacity", action="append", default=[],
                        metavar="Name=N",
                        help="give a component, buffer, relation or "
                        "archetype another capacity, as `capacity Name N` "
                        "after the program would (again for each)")
    parser.add_argument("--touch-keys", metavar="keys",
                        help="with --web: keys drawn on the page where it is "
                        "shown on a touch screen (a phone), which press the "
                        "program's keys: `arrows` or `wasd` (a pad of four), "
                        "Enter, Space, Escape, Tab, Shift, Backspace, A to Z, "
                        "0 to 9; apart by commas or spaces")
    parser.add_argument("--check-externs", action="store_true",
                        help="check as the program runs that each extern fn "
                        "gives the same result for the same arguments, and "
                        "say which extern fns the build could not check")
    parser.add_argument("--keep", metavar="dir", help="keep the generated "
                        "files (IR, header) in this directory")
    parser.add_argument("--no-cache", action="store_true",
                        help="build anew, whether or not the program was "
                        "built before, and do not keep what is built")
    own, rest = sys.argv[1:], []
    if "--" in own:
        rest = own[own.index("--") + 1:]
        own = own[:own.index("--")]
    arguments = parser.parse_args(own)
    if arguments.instrument and arguments.profile:
        parser.error("--instrument and --profile are for two builds")
    if arguments.touch_keys and not arguments.web:
        sys.exit("ent: --touch-keys: for a web page (--web)")

    stem = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(arguments.program))[0]
    if arguments.web:
        if (arguments.gpu or arguments.ranks or arguments.windows
                or arguments.parallel or arguments.instrument
                or arguments.check_externs):
            sys.exit("ent: --web: on one thread, not with --parallel, --gpu, "
                     "--ranks, --windows, --instrument or --check-externs")
        if arguments.command == "run":
            sys.exit("ent run --web: build it (ent build --web) and open "
                     "the .html through a web server")
        arguments.touch_keys = touch_keys(arguments.touch_keys)
        # (A page is three files: not kept as one in the cache.)
        arguments.no_cache = True
        # (A name.js alone, without the page, runs under Node.js too.)
        if not (arguments.output or "").endswith((".html", ".js")):
            arguments.output = (arguments.output or stem) + ".html"
    # A program that is run from here is told where its source is, and
    # that of the modules it imports: the files they name are looked for
    # there too (devices/ent_files.h).
    where = dict(os.environ)
    if os.path.isfile(arguments.program):
        sources = made_of(arguments)[0]
        folders = []
        for path in sources:
            if os.path.dirname(path) not in folders:
                folders.append(os.path.dirname(path))
        where["ENT_PROGRAM_DIR"] = os.pathsep.join(folders)
    # A program that was built from just this is taken as it is. (Not
    # where the generated files are asked for: those come from building.)
    key = None
    if not arguments.no_cache and not arguments.keep:
        if not os.path.isfile(arguments.program):
            sys.exit(f"ent: cannot find '{arguments.program}'")
        key = cache_key(arguments)
    if key:
        kept, there = cached(key, stem)
        if there:
            os.utime(os.path.dirname(kept))
            if arguments.command == "build":
                shutil.copy2(kept, arguments.output or stem)
                copy_assets(arguments, arguments.output or stem)
                return 0
            return subprocess.run(launch(arguments, kept) + rest,
                                  env=where).returncode

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as temporary:
        directory = temporary
        if arguments.keep:
            directory = arguments.keep
            os.makedirs(directory, exist_ok=True)
        if arguments.command == "build":
            output = arguments.output or stem
            build(arguments, output, directory)
            copy_assets(arguments, output)
            if key:
                keep_in_cache(output, key, stem)
            return 0
        executable = os.path.join(temporary, stem)
        build(arguments, executable, directory)
        if key:
            executable = keep_in_cache(executable, key, stem)
        return subprocess.run(launch(arguments, executable) + rest,
                              env=where).returncode


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
